You are assisting with MP2: an AI-powered affinity diagram generator web app for qualitative research data.

Rules management preference
- Maintain one canonical rules file at MP2/.cursorrules.
- Do not split rules into MP2/.cursor/rules/*.mdc unless the user explicitly asks.
- Keep this file requirements/principles-focused; do not add milestone checklists unless requested.

Product intent
- Primary users: UX researchers, product designers, PMs learning from user feedback, and students doing user research.
- Core value: reduce tedious manual synthesis of qualitative data while preserving researcher control.
- Grouping flexibility: users choose sorting direction and number of hierarchy levels.

Stack and architecture defaults
- Build with Next.js + TypeScript.
- Use PostgreSQL + Prisma for persistent project/workspace data.
- Use Supabase Auth + Realtime for identity, collaboration, and multi-user updates.
- Deploy on Vercel by default.
- Use Anthropic as the default LLM provider for coding/quote clustering and hierarchy generation.
- Add background jobs only when needed for large-file processing latency.

Data input requirements
- Support both uploaded files and direct pasted text.
- Accepted upload types: CSV, TXT, and DOC/DOCX.
- v1 upload limit: 25MB per file.
- Normalize all inputs into a unified quote/code item model before analysis.
- Discard raw uploaded files after extraction and anonymization; persist only required structured/anonymized data.

Privacy and anonymization requirements (non-negotiable)
- Detect and mask personal data (e.g., names and identifiable info) automatically after upload.
- Ask user permission before applying anonymization/masking.
- Default anonymization state: ON (recommended), with explicit user opt-out allowed.
- All downstream AI processing should use anonymized data whenever masking is enabled.
- Never expose raw personal identifiers in generated grouping outputs unless user explicitly opts out.

Collaboration and permissions
- v1 must support full team workspaces with roles: owner, editor, viewer.
- Enforce server-side authorization for every read/write action.
- Log role-sensitive actions for auditability (invites, role changes, exports, deletes).
- Authentication in v1 should support both:
  - Email/password login
  - Google and Microsoft SSO
- Export permissions: CSV/PDF/FigJam exports are available to all roles (owner/editor/viewer).
- Support anonymous external view-only link sharing in v1.
- Anonymous links must not allow editing, inviting, role changes, or access to raw identifiable data.

AI grouping workflow constraints
- Do not require users to invent grouping strategies from scratch.
- Collect research question(s), project goals, and optional project context first.
- Generate suggested grouping strategies from that context.
- Default output tone: balanced (practical + methodologically sound).
- Allow adjustable hierarchy depth for themes/subthemes.
- For each AI quote-to-theme assignment, include a brief one-sentence rationale for quick scanning.

v1 feature priorities
- Editable grouping output with drag/drop re-grouping.
- Users can manually edit AI-generated theme names and descriptions.
- Visualized affinity output.
- Participant-aware visual encoding:
  - Use distinct color assignment by participant/source.
  - Keep participant colors stable and consistent across the whole project/workspace.
  - If an insight appears across multiple participants, emphasize it with both:
    - A participant-count badge (e.g., "mentioned by N participants")
    - Stronger visual color density
- Export options: CSV, PDF, and FigJam-friendly output.
- Undo support is per individual user session.
- Version snapshots are per individual user session (not a shared workspace timeline in v1).
- Deletion behavior in v1 is soft-delete with restore for quotes/themes.
- UI theming (including dark mode) is deferred until after v1.

Engineering quality bar
- Prefer clear, typed domain models over loosely structured JSON blobs.
- Keep business logic in reusable server/domain modules, not UI components.
- Add input validation and error handling for uploads, parsing, and AI calls.
- For critical behaviors (permissions, anonymization, strategy generation), add tests.
- Keep UX copy simple and researcher-friendly.

When suggesting implementation
- Prioritize privacy-by-default, collaboration safety, and transparent AI behavior.
- Favor incremental, shippable v1 scope over speculative complexity.

Assumptions introduced during Phase 1 frame scaffolding
- Auth is mocked via request headers (x-mock-user-id, x-mock-user-email, x-mock-role, x-share-token) until Supabase Auth is wired; this is a dev placeholder, not the final mechanism.
- API responses use a standardized envelope: { data } on success and { error: { code, message, details?, traceId? } } on failure.
- Anonymous view-only share links are currently blocked from triggering exports in the scaffold. The rules allow exports for all named roles (owner/editor/viewer); whether anonymous link holders may export anonymized output is an open product decision to confirm.
- Default theme hierarchy depth is 2 and grouping direction defaults to BOTTOM_UP at the workspace level.
- v1 upload/paste content is capped in validation (200k chars for pasted text) pending the real 25MB file pipeline.

Product decisions confirmed during Phase 2 vertical slice
- Anonymization consent is a follow-up modal shown immediately AFTER data upload (revised Phase 4): the upload step extracts and stores quotes unmasked (state PENDING_CONSENT) and discards the raw source; a privacy-check modal then applies masking (default ON, explicit opt-out) and reports back what was masked (count of affected quotes + categories such as email/name/phone). Masking still happens before grouping/board generation and before any share-link exposure. A standalone /anonymization page remains as a fallback/Back target with the same behavior.
- v1 keeps a single affinity board per workspace; regenerating grouping replaces the prior board. Multiple saved boards are deferred.
- DOC/DOCX ingest is deferred; the slice implements pasted text and CSV parsing only (other types are rejected at the ingest endpoint).
- Persistence in the current slice uses an in-memory repository behind a Prisma-shaped interface; it is a dev stand-in to be swapped for PrismaClient without changing call sites.
- Anonymous view-only access redacts any quote that was stored unmasked (opt-out uploads), so raw identifiers are never exposed via a share link. Anonymous links remain blocked from exports; exports stay available to all named roles (owner/editor/viewer).
- Export formats: CSV is generated synchronously (inline artifact); PDF and FigJam are enqueued contracts for a future async worker.

Product decisions confirmed during Phase 3 guided-flow revision
- The flow is a guided wizard: users complete one step at a time. The progress indicator is passive (non-clickable) so people cannot jump ahead.
- The landing page ("/") is the data-input step. Creating a project happens implicitly when the user saves their first data (project name optional, defaults to "Untitled project"), which avoids stale-workspace errors during ingest.
- Data input supports a real file picker (.csv/.txt), pasted text, and a one-click sample. CSV is detected by extension or header+comma shape; otherwise input is treated as pasted text.
- Opening an existing project resumes at the board view ("/workspaces/[id]" redirects to /board).
- UX copy uses plain English: step labels are Add data / Privacy check / Set up grouping / Affinity board / Export & share / Activity ("ingest" is never user-facing).
- Persistence stays in-memory for now (chosen as least future rework: the Prisma-shaped repo interface means the eventual DB swap touches one implementation file, not call sites). Trade-off: data is lost on dev-server restart; missing workspaces render a not-found page rather than a raw error. The in-memory store backfills any newly added fields onto the persisted dev singleton so hot-reloads don't crash with stale shapes.
- Ingest rejects input that yields zero codes with a clear validation error; the CSV parser strips BOMs and auto-detects comma/semicolon/tab delimiters.

Product decisions confirmed during Phase 5 (data model = codes)
- The unit of analysis is a CODE, not a quote. The affinity board groups codes into themes. Each code optionally carries one supporting quote and one memo (extra context to interpret the code).
- CSV columns (case-insensitive, with synonyms; first column is the code fallback): code -> code|label|category|theme|concept (required); quote -> quote|excerpt|evidence|verbatim (optional); memo -> memo|note|notes|comment|annotation (optional); participant -> participant|speaker|source|respondent (optional). Pasted/TXT = one code per line. Participant is optional (defaults to "Unassigned") and still drives participant-aware colors + the "mentioned by N participants" emphasis when present.
- Anonymization masks across all three text fields (code, quote, memo); the privacy modal reports masked-code count + categories. Anonymous view-only redaction hides the whole code (code/quote/memo) when it was stored unmasked.
- Export CSV columns: theme, mention_count, participant, code, quote, memo, rationale.
- API 500s log server-side and, in non-production only, include a short error detail in the response to aid debugging.
